## [x] Feature specs
// one spec per feature, a vertical slice: book.llmd holds the book's endpoints, service, entity and db mapping
@src/cli.ts
  - main(args: string[])
    - ~without a spec, use the only .llmd file in .llmd/, else say which ones there are
    - without a spec, fold every .llmd file in .llmd/, and say "Nothing to fold" only when none had done steps
    ! "llmd snapshot" with two specs that each have a done step folds both and archives each under its own name

@skills/llmd/SKILL.md
  - specs are one per feature, plus app.llmd for shared setup like startup, the db context and auth
  - a shared file appears in every spec that adds to it, each listing only its own feature's part
  - a step goes in the spec of the feature it's for, even when it touches another feature's files
  - with several specs and none named, use the one the request points to ("the next book step"), else ask
  - describing existing code writes one spec per feature plus app.llmd, each with its own Current state; one spec is fine when there are no clear features

@README.md
  - a "One spec per feature" section with a book example and the shared-file rule
  - the AGENTS.md line points at the Current state of every spec in .llmd/
  - "llmd snapshot" without a name folds every spec; describe writes one spec per feature plus app.llmd

## [x] The LLMD page

// One tab that feels like a chat app: the features (the specs in .llmd/) on the left, the feature's chat in the middle, its spec on the right.
// Each feature is one claude session, run headless, and the agent writes the spec as you talk.
// decided: claude only for now, through "claude -p" with stream-json output; other agents can come later
// decided: no terminals; Plan and Implement share the feature's session, and the Plan prompt keeps it from writing code
// decided: New feature makes the spec file in the background, and the agent writes its steps

@src/agent.ts
  - ~agentCommand(command: string, prompt: string, shell: string) -> string
  - agentArgs(prompt: string, session?: string) -> string[]
    - "-p --output-format stream-json --verbose", allow "llmd snapshot", "--resume <session>" when there is one, then "--" and the prompt
  : Part
    - kind: 'text' | 'tool' | 'error'
    - text: string
  - render(event: any, root: string) -> Part[]
    - an assistant's text as markdown, and a tool call as one line, like "Edit cmd/main.go" or "Bash go build ./..."
    - the result's permission denials as "Not allowed: ...", and its errors
    - nothing for thinking, hooks and tool results

@src/page.ts
  - rootOf(uri: vscode.Uri) -> vscode.Uri
    - moved from extension.ts: the folder holding .llmd/, else the workspace folder, else the spec's folder
  - openPage(uri?: vscode.Uri)
    - show the LLMD page, a webview tab, on that feature when given one
  - newFeature()
    - ask for a name, then create .llmd/<name>.llmd with "# <name>" and the other specs' lang: line, and open its chat
  - send(uri: vscode.Uri, text: string)
    - your message, plus which spec the chat is about and the lines selected in the spec's editor
  - runStep(mode: 'implement' | 'plan', uri: vscode.Uri, step: string)
    - agent.stepPrompt, into the feature's chat
  - ask(uri: vscode.Uri, text: string, prompt: string)
    - save the spec, then run llmd.agentCommand with agent.agentArgs at the project root, resuming the feature's session
    - stream agent.render's parts into the chat, markdown through VS Code's own markdown.api.render
    - keep each finished run's session id and the chat's messages in the workspace state; forget the session when resuming fails
    - one run per feature at a time; Stop ends it
  - initPage(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
    - the LLMD: Open command, a serializer so the page comes back after a reload, and a refresh whenever a spec changes

@media/page.js
  - draw the state the extension posts: the features with their open steps, the chat, and the spec as foldable steps with ▶ Implement, Plan and Edit
  - Enter sends and Shift+Enter starts a new line; while Claude works the button stops it, and Enter keeps your message

@media/page.css
  - features, chat and spec side by side in VS Code's theme colors; the spec hides on narrow windows

@src/extension.ts
  - ~runAgent(mode: 'implement' | 'plan', uri: vscode.Uri, step: string)
  - activate(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
